    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/abraham-crijnssen"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abraham-crijnssen.jpg" alt="Abraham Crijnssen" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/abraham-crijnssen">Abraham Crijnssen</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ABRAHAM_CRIJNSSEN_(F-816).JPEG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Don S. Montgomery, USN(Ret), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>European attempts to settle the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guianas">Guiana</a> coast between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orinoco">Orinoco</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazon">Amazon</a> began in the early seventeenth century, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>, English and French traders built short-lived posts along its rivers. The first lasting colony on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suriname-river">Suriname River</a> was founded in 1650 by settlers sent from Barbados by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-willoughby-5th-baron-willoughby-of-parham">Francis Willoughby</a>, Lord Willoughby of Parham, and by the 1660s this English colony, Willoughbyland, had several dozen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> plantations worked by enslaved Africans. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-anglo-dutch-war">Second Anglo-Dutch War</a> a fleet from the Dutch province of Zeeland under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham-crijnssen">Abraham Crijnssen</a> captured its main fort in February 1667 and renamed it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-zeelandia-suriname">Fort Zeelandia</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-breda-1667">Treaty of Breda</a> of July 1667 confirmed each side in what it held, so the Dutch kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suriname">Suriname</a> while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> kept New Netherland, whose capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-amsterdam">New Amsterdam</a> it had renamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>. Many English planters left with their slaves in the following years. Zeeland struggled to run the colony and sold it in 1682 to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-west-india-company">Dutch West India Company</a>, which in 1683 formed the chartered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/society-of-suriname">Society of Suriname</a> with the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornelis-van-aerssen-van-sommelsdijck">Cornelis van Aerssen van Sommelsdijck</a>, each holding one third. Van Aerssen, the first governor under the Society, made peace with the Indigenous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalina-people">Kalina</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lokono">Lokono peoples</a> in 1686 and imposed discipline on planters and soldiers, until mutinous soldiers killed him in 1688.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amsterdam.jpg" alt="Amsterdam" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Imagen_de_los_canales_conc%C3%A9ntricos_en_%C3%81msterdam.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Andrés Barrios, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In the eighteenth century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suriname">Suriname</a> became one of the most profitable <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> colonies. Several hundred plantations lined the Suriname, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commewijne-river">Commewijne</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cottica-river">Cottica</a> rivers, producing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacao">cacao</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> market, and in the 1750s and 1760s planters financed rapid expansion with loans from Amsterdam merchant houses. The work was done by enslaved Africans, who numbered some 50,000 or more in the later eighteenth century against a few thousand Europeans; harsh punishment, disease and heavy labour caused such high mortality that their numbers were sustained only by constant new imports. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paramaribo">Paramaribo</a> was the commercial and administrative centre, home to planters, officials and a growing population of free people of colour. A notable <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sephardic-jews">Sephardic Jewish</a> community, with its own self-governing settlement at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jodensavanne">Jodensavanne</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suriname-river">Suriname River</a>, owned many plantations. Many enslaved people escaped into the rainforest and formed maroon societies, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ndyuka">Ndyuka</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saramaka">Saramaka</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matawai">Matawai</a>, whose descendants still live in the interior. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> was abolished in 1863, followed by ten years of compulsory contract labour under state supervision, planters recruited indentured labourers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> from 1873 and from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> from 1890. Their descendants, together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">Creoles</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maroons">Maroons</a>, Indigenous peoples and others, make Suriname one of the most diverse countries in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. In the twentieth century bauxite mining replaced agriculture as the main source of exports.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/jean-baptiste-du-casse"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jean-baptiste-du-casse.jpg" alt="Jean-Baptiste du Casse" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/jean-baptiste-du-casse">Jean-Baptiste du Casse</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jean_Baptiste_du_Casse.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Attributed to Hyacinthe Rigaud, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suriname">Suriname</a>'s colonial defence rested on a small garrison paid by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/society-of-suriname">Society of Suriname</a>, militias of colonists and forts commanding the rivers, above all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-zeelandia-suriname">Fort Zeelandia</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paramaribo">Paramaribo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-nieuw-amsterdam">Fort Nieuw-Amsterdam</a>, built between 1734 and 1747 where the Suriname and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commewijne-river">Commewijne</a> rivers meet. The colony repulsed a French attack under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-baptiste-du-casse">Jean-Baptiste du Casse</a> in 1689, but in 1712 a French squadron under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacques-cassard">Jacques Cassard</a> forced the planters to pay a heavy ransom. The longest struggle was against the maroons. Expeditions into the rainforest repeatedly failed to destroy their villages, and the colonial government finally signed peace treaties with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ndyuka">Ndyuka</a> in 1760, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saramaka">Saramaka</a> in 1762 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matawai">Matawai</a> in 1767, recognising their freedom in return for promises to hand back new runaways. When new maroon groups led by Boni attacked plantations in the 1770s, the colony called in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> troops, among them the soldier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-gabriel-stedman">John Gabriel Stedman</a>, formed a corps of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-rangers">Black Rangers</a> from slaves freed for the purpose and built a defensive cordon path around the plantation zone. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> occupied Suriname without serious resistance during the French Revolutionary and Napoleonic Wars, from 1799 to 1802 and from 1804 to 1816. After independence the small Surinamese National Army became a political force: sergeants seized power in 1980, and from 1986 to 1992 the army fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jungle-commando">Jungle Commando</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ronnie-brunswijk">Ronnie Brunswijk</a> in the interior war.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/johan-adolf-pengel"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/johan-adolf-pengel.jpg" alt="Johan Adolf Pengel" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/johan-adolf-pengel">Johan Adolf Pengel</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Minister-President_Pengel_profil_1963_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jack de Nijs for Anefo / Anefo, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/society-of-suriname">Society of Suriname</a> the directors in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> appointed the governor, who ruled with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/court-of-policy">Court of Policy</a>, a council whose members he chose from nominees put forward by the planters. Planters, officials and governors frequently quarrelled over taxes, justice and the costs of defence, and several governors were recalled after bitter disputes. Courts handled civil and criminal cases, and colonial ordinances, the <em>plakaten</em>, regulated the lives of the enslaved while offering them little real protection against their owners. The Jewish community at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jodensavanne">Jodensavanne</a> kept unusual privileges of self-government, first granted in the English period and confirmed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>. After the French conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> in 1795 the Society was dissolved and the colony passed to the state, and after the British occupations it was governed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-hague">The Hague</a> through a governor responsible to the Ministry of Colonies. From 1866 a partly elected colonial legislature, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koloniale-staten">Koloniale Staten</a>, gave a small, wealthy electorate a voice, and universal suffrage followed in 1948. The Charter for the Kingdom of the Netherlands of 1954 made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suriname">Suriname</a> an autonomous country within the kingdom, with its own government and parliament, in which parties largely followed ethnic lines, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">Creole</a>-based <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-party-of-suriname">NPS</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johan-adolf-pengel">Johan Adolf Pengel</a> and the Hindustani-based <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/progressive-reform-party-suriname">VHP</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagernath-lachmon">Jagernath Lachmon</a>. The constitution of 1987 created a republic whose president is elected by the 51-member <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-assembly-of-france">National Assembly</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/narrative-of-a-five-years-expedition"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/narrative-of-a-five-years-expedition.jpg" alt="Narrative of a Five Years Expedition against the Revolted Negroes of Surinam" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/narrative-of-a-five-years-expedition">Narrative of a Five Years Expedition against the Revolted Negroes of Surinam</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bartolozzi_-_John_Gabriel_Stedman_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">John Gabriel Stedman / Francesco Bartolozzi, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suriname">Suriname</a> was among the most important <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> possessions in the Atlantic world, and its plantation profits flowed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> merchant houses, absentee owners and investors. It also became a byword for the brutality of plantation <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-gabriel-stedman">John Gabriel Stedman</a>'s illustrated <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narrative-of-a-five-years-expedition">Narrative of a Five Years Expedition against the Revolted Negroes of Surinam</a></em>, published in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1796 with engravings by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-blake">William Blake</a> among others, shaped European debates on slavery, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voltaire">Voltaire</a> set the scene of a mutilated slave in <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/candide">Candide</a></em> of 1759 near Suriname. Earlier, Maria Sibylla Merian's <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metamorphosis-insectorum-surinamensium">Metamorphosis insectorum Surinamensium</a></em> of 1705, based on her stay in the colony, became a landmark of natural history illustration. The maroon nations of the interior are among the most significant in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>: their eighteenth-century treaties made them, alongside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaican-maroons">Maroons of Jamaica</a>, among the first communities of escaped slaves to win recognised autonomy, and they preserved languages, arts and social institutions of strongly African character that anthropologists have studied closely. The abolition of slavery on 1 July 1863 is commemorated each year as Keti Koti, which is also marked in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>. Indentured migration made Suriname a meeting place of African, Asian, Indigenous and European cultures, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sranan-tongo">Sranan Tongo</a> as a common language beside official Dutch. Migration since the 1970s has created a large Surinamese community in the Netherlands that has shaped Dutch culture and politics.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Into the Present</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/second-world-war.jpg" alt="Second World War" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_101I-646-5188-17,_Flugzeuge_Junkers_Ju_87.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Richard Opitz, CC BY-SA 3.0 de</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suriname">Suriname</a>'s transformation from plantation colony into independent state began with the end of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> and the decline of the plantations in the nineteenth century, as many estates were abandoned and freed people moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paramaribo">Paramaribo</a> or to small farms. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> American troops were stationed in the colony to protect the bauxite mines, which supplied a large share of the aluminium ore used by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. Autonomy under the Charter of 1954 was followed by rapid moves towards independence under Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henck-arron">Henck Arron</a>, and Suriname became an independent republic on 25 November 1975, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johan-ferrier">Johan Ferrier</a> as its first president and a large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> aid settlement. A large part of the population emigrated to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> around independence. On 25 February 1980 sixteen sergeants overthrew the government, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/desi-bouterse">Desi Bouterse</a> as their leader, and in the December murders of 1982 fifteen prominent critics were killed, prompting the Netherlands to suspend aid. The interior war of 1986–1992 against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jungle-commando">Jungle Commando</a> devastated Maroon areas and included the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moiwana-massacre">Moiwana massacre</a> of 1986. Civilian rule returned under the 1987 constitution, and elections in 1991 restored stable democracy. Bouterse was elected president in 2010 and convicted of the December murders in 2019. Today Suriname is a multi-ethnic republic of about 600,000 people that hopes offshore oil discoveries made since 2020 will transform its economy.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suriname">Suriname</a> was one of the most profitable plantation colonies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and a notorious example of Atlantic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, whose maroon nations and later indentured migrants from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> created one of the most diverse societies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, independent since 1975.</em></p>

